    <h1>Self-host n8n on an old PC</h1>
    <div class="meta"><DemoBadge /><span>Sample tutorial for the demo site</span><span>·</span><span>Reading time: placeholder</span></div>

    <p class="lead">n8n is an open-source workflow automation tool. Running it on a spare computer at home is a low-cost way to learn automation and Docker in one project.</p>

    <div class="callout warn"><strong>Demo content.</strong> This page shows the tutorial layout. Check the official n8n docs (<a href="https://docs.n8n.io/hosting/">docs.n8n.io/hosting</a>) for current install steps and licensing before you follow along.</div>

    <h2>What you'll need</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>An old PC or laptop that can run a current 64-bit Linux distribution</li>
      <li>A USB stick to install Linux (Ubuntu Server or Debian are common picks)</li>
      <li>A wired network connection if possible</li>
      <li>About an hour, plus coffee</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Steps</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>Install Linux.</strong> Flash an installer to the USB stick, install the OS, and enable SSH so you can manage the box from your main computer.</li>
      <li><strong>Update the system.</strong>
        <pre><code>sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Install Docker.</strong> Follow Docker's official install guide for your distribution, then confirm it works:
        <pre><code>docker --version
docker run hello-world</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Create a volume and start n8n.</strong>
        <pre><code>docker volume create n8n_data
docker run -d --name n8n --restart unless-stopped \
  -p 5678:5678 \
  -v n8n_data:/home/node/.n8n \
  docker.n8n.io/n8nio/n8n</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Open the editor.</strong> On another computer on the same network, visit <code>http://&lt;your-pc-ip&gt;:5678</code> and create the owner account.</li>
      <li><strong>Build a first workflow.</strong> Try a Schedule trigger that sends you a daily message. Small wins first.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Keep it safe</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep n8n on your home network while you learn. Don't port-forward it to the internet.</li>
      <li>If you want remote access later, use a VPN-style tool or a reverse proxy with HTTPS and strong auth.</li>
      <li>Back up the <code>n8n_data</code> volume and keep Docker images updated.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When it breaks</h2>
    <p>Check the logs first: <code>docker logs n8n</code>. Most beginner issues are a port already in use, a firewall rule, or a typo in the run command.</p>

